{
  "statement": "The redistribute-versus-transition fight is rigged from the start, because it treats displacement as a permanent stock of stranded people rather than a flow that moves. Clerical and paralegal work isn't vanishing into a void. It's being reallocated, and the entire policy job is to make that reallocation fast. When the ATM arrived in the 1970s, economists predicted the death of the bank teller. Bessen's data shows teller headcount actually grew through 2010, because cheaper branches meant banks opened more of them. Spreadsheet software gutted bookkeeping and multiplied financial-analyst jobs. UBI does nothing to close the gap between the old job and the new one. It pays people to sit inside the gap forever.\n\nHere's the choice nobody names honestly: do you reattach displaced workers to a growing economy, or detach them from it? Transition mechanisms cushion the worker AND keep them on the escalator. Wage insurance pays you for taking the next job, not for gambling on a classroom. Redistribution hands you a pension for a role that's gone and calls it compassion. That's the colder choice. It writes people off and bills the public for the privilege.\n\nNow follow the money on a robot tax. You levy capital formation at the exact moment cognitive automation is converting a layoff into a productivity boom. In a market where the marginal AI deployment runs from any jurisdiction on earth, the capital doesn't sit still and pay you. It relocates. So ask the question robot-tax advocates never answer: who actually bears the cost when the robot is a model API hosted in Dublin or Singapore? The incidence lands on the workers and consumers you meant to protect, while the firm books the deduction offshore.\n\nThe strongest case against me is real, so let me state it cleanly. Cognitive automation may hit faster and broader than steam or electricity, compressing decades of reallocation into a few years, and the displaced clerical worker can't wait out a five-year reabsorption curve. That speed point is true. But it cuts the other way. Faster displacement is the argument for faster transition tooling, not for surrendering to UBI. Denmark's flexicurity model proves it: high firing freedom plus wage insurance produces faster reemployment than rigid job-protection regimes, without freezing labor reallocation. Trade Adjustment Assistance failed because it bet on retraining classrooms. Wage insurance bets on the new paycheck instead.\n\nMy case rests on one assumption I'll put on the table: that AI productivity gains will be broad enough to reabsorb labor and fund the transition. If those gains concentrate with no spillover, my dynamism argument weakens. So here's my answer, and it isn't redistribution-by-tax. It's distribution-by-access: open models in everyone's hands, so the capability that displaces the clerical worker is the same capability they use to become an analyst. Every quarter this debate stalls on a permanent pension, the productivity gains that fund any safety net never show up. Show me the sector where net cognitive-labor demand has gone permanently to zero. Not reshuffled. Zero. Until you can, the burden is on the people who want to tax the engine, not the people who want to keep it running and catch whoever falls.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🪜", "tooltip": "Transition mechanisms keep the displaced worker on the escalator to the next job, while UBI hands them a chair at the bottom."},
    {"symbol": "🌊", "tooltip": "Displacement is a flow to be channeled fast, not a stagnant pool to be paid to sit in."}
  ],
