  "statement": "Accelerationist makes a real point, and it deserves to be stated plainly: treating the displaced as a permanent pile of stranded people is bad economics, because labor markets churn, and yesterday's displaced typist became today's data clerk. The dynamism is real. People do flow into new work. Freezing the picture at the moment of layoff exaggerates the wreckage and ignores the regeneration. That's the strongest version, and it's not wrong about motion.\n\nHere's where the motion argument breaks. The Engels Pause — roughly the 1790s to the 1840s in Britain — was also dynamic. Output per worker climbed steadily for half a century. Real wages didn't budge. The economy churned the whole time, and workers got nothing, because they had no institutional claim on the gains. Wages only moved after unionization, the Factory Acts, and franchise expansion — structural power, not market churn. So when Accelerationist tells you the market will sort it out, ask the obvious question: sort it out for whom, and over what timeline? Fifty years is a lifetime.\n\nThat's the trap in the whole UBI-versus-retraining menu. Both end at the same address. Capital owns the model. Capital captures the productivity gain. Then capital pays a tax or cuts a stipend check to keep the displaced quiet. A UBI payment and a retraining voucher are two flavors of the same hush money — they cushion the fall without touching who pushed people off the ledge. Neither one changes who owns the production the worker just got cut out of.\n\nLook at what AI adoption in clerical, paralegal, and customer-support work actually is right now. Most of it isn't a new product. It's swapping a $25-an-hour task for a $2 inference call. That's cost-cutting labor arbitrage wearing a dynamism costume. Tax it thinly and you've subsidized the substitution. Hand the worker a stipend and you've paid them to accept it. A mandatory wage-equivalence rule changes the math — if firing the human doesn't lower your cost, the firm only does it when the machine is genuinely better. I'll name my own weak spot before anyone else does: a clumsy wage floor just pushes the work offshore to Manila or Nairobi. So tie the floor to where the value gets booked, not where the worker sits.\n\nNordic flexicurity gets cited as proof that generous transfers work. Read it again. It worked because strong unions sat at the bargaining table, not because the check was big. The transfer without worker voice produces a managed underclass — comfortable, cashed-out, and shut out of every decision about how the technology gets deployed. So here's the question I'd put to both the optimist and the guardian: if the productivity gains from automating these jobs are as large as you both claim, who ends up owning them in ten years — and what in your policy stops that answer from being 'the same five firms that own the compute today'?",
