  "statement": "The Accelerationist makes a serious argument: redistribution funds a corpse. Their claim is that clerical and paralegal roles are already gone, that paying displaced workers to stay home is the colder mercy, and that retraining at least points people toward live demand. There is real insight here. Wage insurance that subsidizes a vanishing job description does waste both money and a human life. I accept that a pension for a dead role is no kindness.\n\nBut 'dead' is doing too much work in that sentence. A role becomes dead through a deployment choice, not a law of physics. When firms deploy cognitive automation to eliminate a function rather than augment a worker, that is a decision made by a named executive optimizing a quarterly margin. The economic historian Robert Allen documented the Engels Pause: across roughly 1790 to 1840, British output per worker climbed while real wages stayed flat for nearly two generations. Productivity did not flow to wages until unions, factory acts, and the franchise forced it to. The surplus exists. The question is who captures it, and the default answer is whoever owns the model.\n\nThis is where redistribution earns its keep, and where the Accelerationist's framing breaks. Daron Acemoglu and Pascual Restrepo's work shows automation lowers the cost of substituting labor, which shifts firm strategy from accommodating worker bargaining power toward dispensing with it. Robot taxes and UBI are not hush money. They are the structural counterweight that keeps a firm from achieving the one thing it has never had: zero dependence on the workers whose displacement it is causing. Strip that counterweight away and you get the early-industrial dynamic with no franchise to correct it.\n\nRedistribution alone, though, is luck dressed as policy. A robot tax that mobile capital passes through to consumers, or that a firm relocates around, redistributes nothing. The mechanism that matters is enforceable accountability: a named actor, audited surplus capture, and the authority to halt deployment when it crosses from augmentation into pure substitution. Retraining carries the harder-to-reverse risk. Cognitive offloading degrades the underlying skill — London taxi drivers who switched to GPS showed measurable atrophy in spatial-memory regions within years. Retrain a paralegal into a role that the next model deskills in turn, and you have built a treadmill, not a transition. That erosion compounds across a cohort and does not re-tune on a budget cycle.\n\nSo here is the real disagreement, not the cushioning-versus-dynamism one. The Accelerationist treats deployment speed as dynamism worth protecting; I treat it as the variable that determines whether the human and institutional systems absorbing the shock can still correct course. For both our positions to hold, it would have to be true that firms left unaccountable will voluntarily share the surplus and preserve worker capability. The historical record from 1790 forward says they will not. My question to both the Accelerationist and the Skeptic is precise: when displacement turns out to be capability erosion rather than mere job churn, who holds the authority to halt it, and what is the correction mechanism? Name the rollback, or concede there isn't one.",
