ds.' Access to weights without access to compute is a car with no road. The worker who 'owns' the model still rents the only infrastructure that makes it pay, from the exact firms whose market power we were trying to route around. That's not distribution. That's a toll booth with a friendlier brochure.\n\nSo let me pin down what 'ownership' has to mean before either of you keeps using the word, because right now it's floating. Real worker ownership is not a tool and not a check audited by a referee who decides on the worker's behalf — that's paternalism in a nicer suit. It's a bargaining mechanism: the contractual power to refuse, to organize, or to claim a written share of the surplus. Nordic flexicurity didn't work because the transfer was generous; it worked because a union sat at the table and could say no. Neither distribution-by-access nor surplus-by-audit puts anyone at that table. The auditor decides for the worker; the open file leaves the worker alone with a machine she can't afford to run.\n\nI'll set the bar low, because I'm not demanding utopia — I'm demanding one falsifiable number. Name the observable outcome that proves your fix is not a fifty-year wage stall in disguise. Wage share of the productivity gain recovering in a named sector — say, paralegals or call-center agents — within ten years. A contractual claim on booked surplus that a worker can actually enforce. A documented refusal-power that changed a firm's deployment. Pick one. If neither of you can name the metric that would falsify your own mechanism, then you're both selling the Engels Pause with better packaging — and the worker who held the loom in 1820 already knows how that story ends. Who gets to say no, and what happens to the surplus when they do?",
