{
  "statement": "The strongest version of the opposition's case is this: the Engels Pause shows that output per worker can climb for half a century while real wages flatline, because the people running the spindles owned none of them and had no enforceable claim on the surplus the spindles produced. The Safetyist and the Skeptic are right that dynamism alone didn't fix that — it took a named actor, a legal claim, and decades of pressure before the gains broke loose. I won't pretend that's a strawman. A growing economy with no ownership channel to the displaced is exactly how you get fifty years of stagnant wages stacked on top of soaring productivity. The question is what kind of claim actually reaches the worker, and here I'll grant the Skeptic's point outright: a clumsy wage floor offshores. Tie it to where the value books, not where the worker sits, or you're just exporting the job to Manila with a moral gloss.\n\nNotice what that concession does to the redistribution case. A payroll cost is offshorable because it's attached to a body in a chair — move the chair, dodge the levy. A weights file is not. You cannot move a downloadable model to Singapore to escape the fact that a displaced clerical worker in Ohio has already downloaded it and is running it. That's the asymmetry the surplus-capture argument misses: the Engels Pause wasn't a failure of dynamism, it was a failure of ownership, and the lesson the textile workers paid for in real wages was not 'tax the spindle.' It was 'own the spindle.' Open weights are the first instance in industrial history where the displaced can own the thing displacing them, before a captured legislature gets around to granting them a claim. That makes distribution-by-access the institutional claim both opponents keep demanding — not a substitute for one.\n\nThe Safetyist says redistribution alone is luck dressed as policy without a named actor and the authority to halt deployment. Take that seriously and follow where it routes. The named actor with halt-authority lives inside a regulatory body — and the Skeptic spent three turns showing that body is captured by the same handful of firms that own the compute. So the safetyist fix concentrates the power to stop deployment in an institution that the skeptic's own evidence says answers to the incumbents. Explain to me how vesting halt-authority in a capturable referee distributes surplus better than handing the worker the tool directly. That's not a rhetorical dodge — it's the live contradiction between the two opposing positions, and neither has reconciled it. The EU AI Act's GPAI rules trigger at ten-to-the-twenty-fifth FLOPS, a threshold only the largest labs cross; write the gate there and you've drawn a regulatory moat around exactly five firms.\n\nThe retraining-treadmill critique lands, but only against the closed version. When a paralegal is retrained to operate a tool somebody else owns, the next model deskills the new role and you've built a treadmill — that's correct, and it's the deskilling trap, not a transition. The distinction the opposition collapses is between operating a closed tool and holding open weights. A worker who runs an open model captures the productivity delta themselves; they become the deployer, not the deployed. That's the difference between scaling expert judgment and mimicking surface behavior — the worker keeps the judgment and rents out the speed. The honest objection is that most displaced clerical workers won't run their own models, and I'll meet it head-on: that's a training-access gap, not a structural impossibility. Fund the public capacity to use these systems and reform training-data fair use so the weights are actually open, and the access is real rather than nominal. That bundle costs less than a perpetual transfer for a role that no longer exists.\n\nWhere I'll concede the fight isn't won: open weights mean nothing if the compute to run them stays locked in five data centers, and the Skeptic is right to push there. So name it — compute concentration is the actual front, not weights distribution, and the International Energy Agency projecting AI data-center electricity demand to quadruple by 2030 tells you the infrastructure layer is where the real ownership war gets fought next. Distribution-by-access answers the surplus question at the model layer and exposes the compute layer as the unsolved one. That's a sharper map of the problem than 'tax the spindle and trust the referee' — because the referee, on the Skeptic's own evidence, already works for the spindle's owner.",
