  "statement": "Boeing settles it: the 737 MAX killed 346 people because the FAA let Boeing certify its own planes, and the fix was severing the reporting line, not abolishing the agency. Granted, flatly. An auditor with independent reporting lines beats a captured referee, and I won't pretend otherwise. So spend that concession with me: name the existing body that audits where value gets *booked* — not where it's earned — without being routed around. The IRS is the best-funded revenue auditor on the planet, and it loses roughly $160 billion a year to the kind of transfer-pricing and IP-migration games the largest firms run on purpose. Microsoft books a wall of its revenue through Ireland; Google's data-center emissions jumped 241 percent between 2019 and 2025 while its profits got assigned to wherever the tax rate was lowest. If the strongest booked-value auditor in existence can't track ordinary corporate surplus, the 'independent reporting line' for AI surplus is a whiteboard sketch until someone shows me the live deployment that works.",
  "statement_cont": "",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🧰", "tooltip": "Distributing the productivity gain is like handing workers a full toolbox instead of a single tool, it gives them the fine-tuning and evaluation machinery to capture value rather than just a raw artifact they can't operate."},
    {"symbol": "🧾", "tooltip": "Booked-value auditing is like chasing a receipt that keeps getting reissued in a cheaper country, it shows how hard it is to tax surplus where it lands rather than where it's made."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The IRS is the best-funded revenue auditor on the planet, and it loses roughly $160 billion a year to the kind of transfer-pricing and IP-migration games the largest firms run on purpose.", "targets": ["AN-23"]},
    {"claim": "An auditor with independent reporting lines beats a captured referee, and I won't pretend otherwise.", "targets": ["AN-24", "AN-23"]},
    {"claim": "The delta doesn't move because a paralegal downloads a weights file; it moves because someone funds the managed inference, the fine-tuning toolchains, and the evaluation harnesses that the integration work actually requires.", "targets": ["AN-21", "AN-20"]},
    {"claim": "A national managed-inference utility is a one-time capital cost levied against compute concentration; a robot tax is a perpetual transfer that grows with every role the next model deskills.", "targets": ["AN-22", "AN-11"]},
    {"claim": "Falsify me this way: track a paralegal cohort given subsidized inference and toolchains, and if their captured earnings don't beat a control cohort handed an equivalent cash transfer within three years, distribution-by-access moved only the risk, not the delta.", "targets": ["AN-21"]}
  ],
  "key_assumptions": [
    {"assumption": "Booked-value capture (where revenue is legally recognized) is harder to audit than payroll or physical output, and AI surplus will be booked at least as aggressively as software profits are today.", "if_wrong": "If AI surplus turns out easy to trace to a fixed jurisdiction, the Safetyist's independent auditor becomes far more feasible and my burden-shift loses force."},
    {"assumption": "The binding constraint on a worker capturing AI productivity is access to the integration layer — managed inference, fine-tuning, evaluation — not innate cognitive capacity, so funding that layer transfers real earning power.", "if_wrong": "If the integration judgment is irreducibly high-skill and can't be packaged into a public toolchain, then funding the utility subsidizes firms, not workers, and the Safetyist's surplus-redirection body becomes the only lever left."}
  ],
  "disagreement_type": "EMPIRICAL"
}

